{"id":"6e725575-d946-477d-b095-c3e3d2495921","object":"chat.completion","created":1785006121,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    nodes_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        nodes_by_id[nid] = node\\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    \\n    retained = set(nodes_by_id.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    \\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    \\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if symbol_counts[sym] == 1 and sym in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    \\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The case contains nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility, and a required_symbols list. The objective is to perform grammar-guided node reduction: we want to remove removable nodes while preserving that all required_symbols remain represented (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains? Or each required_symbol must be present as a symbol in the tree after removal?). Also, we must ensure that the root is never removed, and nodes with retained children? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained (i.e., not removed). So removal is only possible for leaf nodes (or nodes whose children have all been removed). Also, \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So before deciding to remove a node, we check if after removal, every required_symbol still exists in the tree. We need to iterate deeper nodes before shallower nodes (i.e., process nodes by descending depth). At equal depth, use node ID as tie break (stable). We need to return removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). The output schema specifies arrays of strings for both.\n\nGiven the fixture payloads, the tree structure: nodes have parent (null for root). Root depth 0. We need to build parent-child relationships. The algorithm:\n\n1. Build a map from node id to node dict.\n2. Build a map from parent id to list of child ids (or directly set children).\n3. Determine the set of required symbols.\n4. Initialize retained set with all nodes that are not removable? Actually we will start with all nodes retained, then attempt to remove removable nodes in order: deeper first, then ID tiebreak. For each candidate node (must be removable and not root and not a required symbol? Actually \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" meaning if the node's symbol is in required_symbols? Or the node itself is required? The node has a symbol, and we need to preserve representation of required symbols. So if the node's symbol is required, we cannot remove it, because that would remove that required symbol. But maybe there are multiple nodes with same required symbol, then removing one might be okay if at least one remains. The instruction: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies we check after removal of that node, if all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains). So we cannot remove a node if it is the last node of a required symbol.\n\nAlso, \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So before considering a node, we must ensure all its children have been removed (i.e., are not in retained set). Because if children are retained, we cannot remove the parent. So we process nodes in depth order from deepest to shallowest. This ensures children are considered before parents.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n- Create a set of all node ids.\n- Create a set of required symbols.\n- Function is_required_symbol_covered(set_of_symbols): after removal, check that each required_symbol is present in the set of symbols of retained nodes.\n- Start with retained_ids = set of all node ids.\n- Get list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then id ascending (for tie break). For each node:\n  - Skip if not removable, or if node id is root (parent null? Actually root has parent null and removable false anyway), or if node's symbol is in required_symbols? No, we check overall coverage later.\n  - Check if node has any children that are still retained. For that, we need to know parent-child relationships: we can precompute children list.\n  - If children_retained > 0, skip (cannot remove parent with retained children).\n  - Compute the set of symbols if we remove this node: symbols of retained_ids minus {node.symbol}. But we must also consider that other nodes in the tree may have that symbol. We can precompute a symbol count per symbol from retained_ids. Initially count all symbols. When considering removal, we would decrement count for that symbol. If after decrement, any required_symbol count becomes 0, then removal is not allowed.\n  - If allowed, we remove the node: remove from retained_ids, record in removed_node_ids list (order of removal), and update symbol counts (decrement).\n- After processing all nodes, the retained_node_ids is sorted id strings (ascending) of retained_ids.\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nWe must be careful: children are defined by parent field. We need to build children map from parent id to list of child ids. Also, root has parent null.\n\nEdge cases: if there is no removable node or all removable nodes can be removed, that's fine. The fixture shows exactly one root, and some removable nodes. The required_symbols lists include \"root\" and \"s1\". Root symbol \"root\" always present. s1 appears in nodes n1 (depth1) and n4 (depth2). So at least one s1 must remain. In the first fixture, n1 is removable? No, n1 removable false. n4 removable true. So if we remove n4, s1 still present from n1. So removal allowed. Check n2 removable true, its children n4 (removable) and maybe none else. If we remove n2 before n4? But n2 has child n4 retained? We process deeper first: n4 depth2, n2 depth1, so n4 will be considered first. After removing n4, n2 becomes leaf? But n2 might have other children? In fixture, n2 only child n4. So after removing n4, n2 has no retained children, so n2 can be removed if allowed: n2 symbol s2, not required, and after removal, s1 still present, root present, s0 present? required is root and s1, so allowed. So removal order: n4 then n2. n6 is depth2, removable true, parent n1, but n1 is non-removable, and n6 symbol s0, not required. Removing n6 is allowed if s0 still present? s0 appears in n3 (depth1 non-removable) and n6 (removable). Removing n6 still leaves s0 from n3. Allowed. So removal order: n4, n6, n2 (since depth 2 first, then depth1). That matches expected? Not provided but plausible.\n\nWe need to ensure tie break at equal depth uses node ID (string) ascending. So if two nodes of same depth, we use string comparison.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- The case payload is a dict with keys \"nodes\" (list of dicts) and \"required_symbols\" (list of strings).\n- Build nodes_by_id dict.\n- Build children: for each node, if parent is not None, add child id to parent's children list.\n- Build initial symbol_counts: dict from symbol to count of nodes with that symbol (all nodes).\n- retained_ids = set of all node ids.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending.\n- removed = [] (list)\n- For node in sorted_nodes:\n  - if not node[\"removable\"]: continue\n  - if node[\"id\"] is root? root is node with parent None, but we can also check depth==0 or id == \"root\"? better to check parent None.\n  - if node[\"parent\"] is None: continue (root is not removable in fixture anyway)\n  - Check if any child in children[node[\"id\"]] is in retained_ids: if yes, continue.\n  - Check if after removal, required symbols still covered:\n    - symbol = node[\"symbol\"]\n    - if symbol_counts[symbol] - 1 == 0 and symbol in required_symbols_set: not allowed.\n  - If allowed:\n    - removed.append(node[\"id\"])\n    - retained_ids.remove(node[\"id\"])\n    - symbol_counts[symbol] -= 1\n- After loop, retained_sorted = sorted(retained_ids)\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nNote: We must not mutate case. We can copy or just use data.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that returns the dict.\n\nNow, we need to output JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON string. The source must be valid Python. We'll write it as a multiline string, escaping newlines and quotes.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer as a JSON object with \"implementation\" key and value being the Python code string.\n\nMake sure to escape properly: newlines -> \\n, backslashes -> \\\\, quotes -> \\\".\n\nLet's write the Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    \n    # Build maps\n    nodes_by_id = {}\n    children = {}\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        nodes_by_id[nid] = node\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    \n    retained = set(nodes_by_id.keys())  # all nodes initially retained\n    removed = []  # order of removal\n    \n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # never remove root\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            continue\n        # cannot remove if any child is retained\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # check required symbols coverage\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        # simulate removal: if this is the only node with that symbol and symbol is required, disallow\n        if symbol_counts[sym] == 1 and sym in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # remove\n        removed.append(nid)\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n```\n\nWe must handle case where nodes may have missing children? Already handled.\n\nEdge: if a node is removable but its parent is removed later? That's fine because we process deeper first, so children are removed before parents. But if a child is not removable, parent cannot be removed anyway.\n\nNow, produce final JSON output.\n\nWe need to ensure the implementation is a single string with proper escaping. We'll use json.dumps to format it, but we must output the JSON with key \"implementation\" and value as a string. So we'll construct the Python dict and then json.dumps it.\n\nWe'll write the final answer as a JSON object with \"implementation\" key. The response must be exactly that JSON object.\n\nLet's produce."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":2782,"total_tokens":4951,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2412},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}